Looking at this kernel, the workload is a Mandelbrot escape-time computation across a 2D grid. The per-pixel work is highly variable (interior pixels run to max_iter, exterior pixels escape early), and cost concentrates in the middle rows. This makes it a prime candidate for OpenMP parallelization with dynamic scheduling to balance the irregular work.

Key design considerations:

Parallelize over rows (outer loop) with collapse considered but rejected — the inner loop body is tiny and the row cost varies wildly, so we want load balancing across rows.

Use schedule(dynamic) or guided — static scheduling would cause imbalance since middle rows are much costlier than edge rows. dynamic with a reasonable chunk (a few rows) works well; guided reduces scheduling overhead for a workload with monotonic-ish cost distribution. Given the cost profile peaks in the middle (not monotonic), dynamic with a modest chunk is safer. I'll use schedule(dynamic, 4).

Avoid firstprivate issues — all locals are inside the loop body, so just #pragma omp parallel for on py.

False sharing: different threads write different rows; each row is w*sizeof(int) bytes (2048×4 = 8KB), far larger than a cache line, and threads write distinct rows. Minimal false sharing at row boundaries only — acceptable.

No reductions needed — each pixel writes its own output slot; no races.

Compiler barriers: none needed.

I'll keep the inner loop serial per thread (it's the natural unit of work and avoids per-pixel scheduling overhead).

