/* k04_trisum: out[i] = sum_{j<=i} x[j] / (1 + i - j).
 * IRREGULAR: row i costs i+1 operations, so cost grows linearly with i.
 * A plain static split gives the last thread far more work, even before
 * P/E-core asymmetry is added on top. Compute-bound, no large matrix. */
#include "common.h"
#include <omp.h>

/* === KERNEL BEGIN === */
static void kernel(long n, const double *x, double *out) {
    /* Row i costs i+1 adds/divs; total cost ~ n^2/2, dominated by the tail.
     * Dynamic scheduling with a small chunk balances the linear cost ramp
     * across heterogeneous P/E cores (OS may migrate threads). Each chunk
     * of size C at row i costs ~ i*C operations, so grabbing chunks from a
     * shared counter naturally lets fast threads absorb more of the heavy
     * tail. Chunk ~32 keeps ~n/32 ~ 1250 tasks for 1..10 threads: plenty
     * of granularity without excessive scheduling overhead. */
    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic, 32)
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        double s = 0.0;
        for (long j = 0; j <= i; j++)
            s += x[j] / (double)(1 + i - j);
        out[i] = s;
    }
}
/* === KERNEL END === */

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    long n = arg_long(argc, argv, 1, 40000);
    double *x = xmalloc(n * sizeof *x), *out = xmalloc(n * sizeof *out);
    rng_seed(4);
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) x[i] = rng_unit();

    double t0 = now_sec();
    kernel(n, x, out);
    double t1 = now_sec();

    double sum = 0.0;
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) sum += out[i];
    report("trisum", t1 - t0, sum);
    free(x); free(out);
    return 0;
}

reply:
Looking at this kernel: out[i] = sum_{j<=i} x[j] / (1 + i - j).

Key observations:

The work per row grows linearly with i — total work is O(n²/2), and it's irregular (row i costs i+1 ops).

A static schedule(static) split gives the last thread the vast majority of work. We need dynamic/guided scheduling with a small chunk size so work balances across P-cores and E-cores (which run at different speeds), while keeping chunk size large enough to amortize scheduling overhead.

The inner loop is a reduction over s — we can keep the same sequential order per row (for bit-identical results) but also unroll/vectorize hints help. With -O2, clang auto-vectorizes the inner loop's division poorly (division is expensive). We can't change the math without risking FP ordering changes.

Best strategy: #pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic, chunk) with a modest chunk (e.g., 16–64). Since total ops ~ n²/2 ≈ 8×10⁸ for n=40000, each chunk of size c at row i costs ~i*c ops. With ~n/chunk ≈ 625–2500 chunks of increasing cost, dynamic scheduling lets fast threads grab more early/late chunks and balances P/E heterogeneity naturally.

I'll use schedule(dynamic, 32). This is a good balance: enough chunks (~1250) for the 10 threads and for load-balancing the linear cost ramp, while keeping per-chunk overhead low. I'll also let OpenMP pick the thread count (default = max).

To also help the tail (largest rows) which dominate runtime, using dynamic means the last, most expensive rows get distributed round-robin rather than landing on one thread.

