Looking at this kernel, the key challenge is load balancing: row i costs i+1 operations, so the total work is n(n+1)/2 — triangular. A naive static split gives the last thread much more work. The classic fix is to assign iterations in a way that pairs up rows i and n-1-i (whose costs sum to n), giving each "block" equal cost. I'll use schedule(dynamic) with a good chunk size, or better, schedule(guided) which balances well for triangular work without excessive synchronization. Combined with per-thread private accumulation of s, and letting OpenMP handle the reduction-free write to out[i] (each i written once, no race).

Using schedule(guided) gives near-optimal load balance for this irregular loop, and nowait-free parallel for is fine. I'll also collapse nothing (there's no inner parallelizable loop). This gives good scaling across 1–10 threads.

